#--: Error codes

Every error the compiler reports has a code, #~"E" and four digits, and the
first line of a diagnostic names it (#~"error[E0200]: ..."). The codes are
grouped by range: [#> to="./zc.html#error-code-numbering": Error Code Numbering]
says which range is which stage. #~"zc explain E0200" prints a code's
meaning and a longer description.

The table gives each code's meaning, as #~"zc explain" gives it. A section
for each code follows, with a program that the compiler refuses with that
code; the documentation's checks compile each one and require that code as
its first error.

#table:
  [#tr: [#th: Code] [#th: Meaning]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0002"] [#td: file not found]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0003"] [#td: duplicate definition]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0004"] [#td: bad unit name]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0005"] [#td: malformed unit]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0007"] [#td: I/O error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0009"] [#td: unexpected characters]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0010"] [#td: expected a label]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0012"] [#td: expected an expression]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0013"] [#td: expected a value]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0014"] [#td: expected a type definition]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0015"] [#td: expected a statement]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0017"] [#td: malformed argument]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0019"] [#td: malformed expression]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0021"] [#td: malformed string]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0022"] [#td: malformed path]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0023"] [#td: malformed reference]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0025"] [#td: malformed parameter]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0026"] [#td: malformed parameter block]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0027"] [#td: malformed object block]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0028"] [#td: malformed item]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0029"] [#td: malformed then clause]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0031"] [#td: malformed case clause]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0032"] [#td: malformed for header]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0033"] [#td: reserved word used as a name]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0034"] [#td: malformed list]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0035"] [#td: malformed statement]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0036"] [#td: non-exhaustive match]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0037"] [#td: reserved member name]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0039"] [#td: no main]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0040"] [#td: malformed main]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0041"] [#td: not data in a data file]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0042"] [#td: nested or chained too deep]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0100"] [#td: type error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0101"] [#td: member not public]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0102"] [#td: private escape]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0103"] [#td: used before it is initialised]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0200"] [#td: ownership error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0300"] [#td: call error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0400"] [#td: generic error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0500"] [#td: emitter error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0501"] [#td: no native implementation]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0600"] [#td: compile-time evaluation error]]
  [#tr: [#td: #~"E0601"] [#td: compile-time error]]


#--: E0002

#b:"file not found." The source file of a unit could not be found under the source roots or the system library. A single file cannot show it: #~"zc build nosuch", in a directory with no #~"nosuch.z", reports it.

#code: """
  $ zc build nosuch
  error[E0002]: file not found
  """


#--: E0003

#b:"duplicate definition." Two definitions in one scope share a name.

#code language=zerolang error=E0003: """
  limit: 5
  limit: 6

  main: function is {
      print "\{limit}"
  }
  """


#--: E0004

#b:"bad unit name." A unit's name is not an identifier. A file's name gives its unit's name, so a file cannot show it: a file named #~"bad-name.z" is refused.

#code: """
  $ zc build bad-name
  error[E0004]: Invalid unit name
  """


#--: E0005

#b:"malformed unit." Something other than a definition stands at the top level of a unit: here a closing brace with no opening one.

#code language=zerolang error=E0005: """
  main: function is { print "a" }
  }
  """


#--: E0007

#b:"I/O error." Reading or writing a file failed: a source root that is not a folder, a file that cannot be read. A single file cannot show it.


#--: E0009

#b:"unexpected characters." The characters form no token of the language: here a run of two dots.

#code language=zerolang error=E0009: """
  main: function is {
      x: 1 .. 2
      print "\{x}"
  }
  """


#--: E0010

#b:"expected a label." #~"for each" and #~"with" give the value they bind a name with a label. Here #~"for each" has no name for the element.

#code language=zerolang error=E0010: """
  main: function is {
      xs: ListVal i64
      for each xs.iterate loop print "x"
  }
  """


#--: E0012

#b:"expected an expression." The parser expected an expression: here a #~"with" label with no value.

#code language=zerolang error=E0012: """
  main: function is {
      with x: do print "a"
  }
  """


#--: E0013

#b:"expected a value." The condition of an #~"if" clause or a #~"for" loop, the subject of a #~"match", and the value a #~"for" binding label names are each required. Here an #~"if" has no condition.

#code language=zerolang error=E0013: """
  main: function is {
      if then {
          print "x"
      }
  }
  """


#--: E0014

#b:"expected a type definition." The parser expected a type definition or a value: here a label with nothing after it.

#code language=zerolang error=E0014: """
  x:
  main: function is { print "a" }
  """


#--: E0015

#b:"expected a statement." The parser expected an expression in a body: here a closing parenthesis.

#code language=zerolang error=E0015: """
  main: function is {
      if true then )
  }
  """


#--: E0017

#b:"malformed argument." A function's signature is not well formed: here it declares both #~"out" and #~"outx".

#code language=zerolang error=E0017: """
  mk: function out i64 outx i64 is {
      return 1
  }

  main: function is {
      print "\{mk}"
  }
  """


#--: E0019

#b:"malformed expression." An expression is not well formed: here a parenthesis that does not close.

#code language=zerolang error=E0019: """
  main: function is {
      x: (1 + 2
      print "\{x}"
  }
  """


#--: E0021

#b:"malformed string." A string literal is not well formed: here an escape the language does not have.

#code language=zerolang error=E0021: """
  main: function is {
      s: "bad \q escape"
      print s
  }
  """


#--: E0022

#b:"malformed path." A path is not well formed: here a field read after #~".take", which ends a path.

#code language=zerolang error=E0022: """
  pt: class { a: i64 }

  main: function is {
      p: pt a: 3
      q: p.take.a
      print "\{q}"
  }
  """


#--: E0023

#b:"malformed reference." A name refers to nothing in scope.

#code language=zerolang error=E0023: """
  main: function is {
      print missing
  }
  """


#--: E0025

#b:"malformed parameter." A parameter is not well formed: here two parameters share a name.

#code language=zerolang error=E0025: """
  f: function {a: i64 a: i64} is { print "a" }

  main: function is { f a: 1 }
  """


#--: E0026

#b:"malformed parameter block." A parameter block is not well formed: here it does not close with a brace.

#code language=zerolang error=E0026: """
  f: function {a: i64 )} is { print "a" }

  main: function is { f a: 1 }
  """


#--: E0027

#b:"malformed object block." An object body is not well formed: here a record's body does not close.

#code language=zerolang error=E0027: """
  R: record {
      a: i64

  main: function is { print "a" }
  """


#--: E0028

#b:"malformed item." An item of a definition is not well formed: here a variant is #~"is native" and has an is-block too.

#code language=zerolang error=E0028: """
  tri: variant is native { lo: null mid: null }

  main: function is {
      t: tri.lo
      match (t) case lo then print "lo" case mid then print "mid"
  }
  """


#--: E0029

#b:"malformed then clause." A #~"then" clause is not well formed: here it holds a bare #~"if", which needs braces.

#code language=zerolang error=E0029: """
  main: function is {
      a: 0
      b: 5
      if b > 3 then if b > 99 then a = 1 else a = 2
      print "a=\{a}"
  }
  """


#--: E0031

#b:"malformed case clause." A #~"match" is not well formed: here it has no #~"case".

#code language=zerolang error=E0031: """
  color: variant {
      red: null
      green: null
  }

  k: match color.red

  main: function is {
      print "done"
  }
  """


#--: E0032

#b:"malformed for header." A #~"for" header is not well formed: here it has no condition and no #~"loop".

#code language=zerolang error=E0032: """
  main: function is {
      for { print "a" }
  }
  """


#--: E0033

#b:"reserved word used as a name." A reserved word is used as a name: here the parameter #~"take".

#code language=zerolang error=E0033: """
  f: function {take: i64} out i64 is { return take }

  main: function is { print "\{f take: 1}" }
  """


#--: E0034

#b:"malformed list." A list is not well formed: here an empty list that does not name its type.

#code language=zerolang error=E0034: """
  none: list {}

  main: function is {
      print "\{none.length}"
  }
  """


#--: E0035

#b:"malformed statement." A statement is not well formed: here a bare #~"if" as the argument of #~"return".

#code language=zerolang error=E0035: """
  pick: function {c: bool} out i64 is {
      return if c then 1 else 2
  }

  main: function is {
      print "\{pick c: true}"
  }
  """


#--: E0036

#b:"non-exhaustive match." A #~"match" handles fewer arms than its subject can hold, and has no #~"else".

#code language=zerolang error=E0036: """
  color: variant {
      red: null
      green: null
  }

  describe: function {c: color} is {
      match (c) case red then print "red"
  }

  main: function is {
      describe c: color.green
  }
  """


#--: E0037

#b:"reserved member name." A member uses a reserved name: here #~"public:", which must introduce a unit definition.

#code language=zerolang error=E0037: """
  box: class {
      public: 0
  }

  main: function is {
      print "unreachable"
  }
  """


#--: E0039

#b:"no main." The program declares no #~"main". The documentation checks its examples as libraries, which need none, so this one is shown and not checked: #~"zc build" of this unit reports it.

#code language=zerolang check=none: """
  double: function {n: i64} out i64 is {
      return n * 2
  }
  """


#--: E0040

#b:"malformed main." #~"main" declares a parameter, and the runtime has none to pass it. A program reads its command line through #~"os.args".

#code language=zerolang error=E0040: """
  main: function {args: (List String).view} is {
      print "n=\{args.length}"
  }
  """


#--: E0041

#b:"not data in a data file." A data file holds something that is not data: here an operator expression. See [#> to=./zdata.html: zdata].

#code language=zdata error=E0041: """
  sum: 1 + 2
  """


#--: E0042

#b:"nested or chained too deep." The source nests or chains deeper than 500 levels. An example would be a line of 500 operations, so none is shown.


#--: E0100

#b:"type error." A type rule is broken: here #~"bool" has no #~"+".

#code language=zerolang error=E0100: """
  main: function is {
      x: true + 1
      print "\{x}"
  }
  """


#--: E0101

#b:"member not public." A member its unit does not make public is read from outside the unit.

#code language=zerolang error=E0101: """
  shapes: unit {
      public: unit { :area }
      area: function out i64 is { return 4 }
      secret: function out i64 is { return 7 }
  }

  main: function is {
      print "\{shapes.secret}"
  }
  """


#--: E0102

#b:"private escape." A parameter takes a #~".private" handle, and a plain value is given.

#code language=zerolang error=E0102: """
  Bag: class { a: i64 count: i64 } as {
      public: unit { :count }
  }

  friendRead: function {h: Bag.private.borrow} out i64 is { return h.a }

  main: function is {
      b: Bag a: 42 count: 3
      print "\{friendRead h: b}"
  }
  """


#--: E0103

#b:"used before it is initialised." A local is used inside its own definition, before it holds a value.

#code language=zerolang error=E0103: """
  main: function is {
      n: n + 1
      print "\{n}"
  }
  """


#--: E0200

#b:"ownership error." An ownership rule is broken: here a string is read after it was moved.

#code language=zerolang error=E0200: """
  main: function is {
      s: "a".string
      t: s.take
      print s
      print t
  }
  """


#--: E0300

#b:"call error." A call is not well formed: here an argument names no parameter.

#code language=zerolang error=E0300: """
  f: function {a: i64} is { print "\{a}" }

  main: function is {
      f b: 1
  }
  """


#--: E0400

#b:"generic error." A generic instantiation is not well formed: here two arguments give the same parameter.

#code language=zerolang error=E0400: """
  main: function is {
      x: ListVal i64 t: f64
  }
  """


#--: E0500

#b:"emitter error." The C backend cannot emit what the program needs: a construct it has no route for, or a unit whose needs the chosen C compiler lacks -- here #~"f128", which tcc does not have. #~"zc build --cc tcc" of this program reports it; the documentation compiles with the default compiler, so it is shown and not checked.

#code language=zerolang check=none: """
  main: function is {
      x: 1.5.f128
      print "\{x}"
  }
  """


#--: E0501

#b:"no native implementation." An #~"is native" declaration has no row in its unit's #~"nativec.zd". A native unit is a #~".z" file and its #~"nativec.zd", so a single file cannot show it; see [#> to=./native_units.html: Native Units].


#--: E0600

#b:"compile-time evaluation error." A value the compiler computes while it compiles cannot be computed: here a division by a constant zero.

#code language=zerolang error=E0600: """
  half: 10 / 0

  main: function is {
      print "\{half}"
  }
  """


#--: E0601

#b:"compile-time error." The program's own #~"error" fires while the compiler decides a condition it can compute.

#code language=zerolang error=E0601: """
  mode: 0

  main: function is {
      if mode == 0 then error "mode must not be zero"
  }
  """
